@@ -0,0 +1,92 @@
+# Conditionable
+
+`@xaravel/conditionable` ports Laravel 13's `Illuminate\Support\Traits\Conditionable` trait and `Illuminate\Support\HigherOrderWhenProxy`. Laravel's documentation uses `when` and `unless` on query builders, collections, strings, requests and many other classes. This page covers the trait those classes share.
+
+## Using the trait
+
+Traits are runtime mixins in Xaravel. Mix `Conditionable` into a class with `mixin` from `@xaravel/runtime`:
+
+```ts
+import { Conditionable } from "@xaravel/conditionable";
+import { mixin, registerClass } from "@xaravel/runtime";
+
+export class Report extends mixin(null, Conditionable) {
+  lines: string[] = [];
+
+  add(line: string): this {
+    this.lines.push(line);
+    return this;
+  }
+}
+
+registerClass("App\\Support\\Report", Report);
+```
+
+`classUsesRecursive(Report)` names the trait by its Laravel FQCN, `Illuminate\Support\Traits\Conditionable`.
+
+## `when` and `unless`
+
+`when(value, callback, defaultValue)` calls `callback(this, value)` when `value` is truthy. Otherwise it calls `defaultValue(this, value)` when one is given. `unless(value, callback, defaultValue)` does the reverse: it calls `callback` when `value` is falsy.
+
+```ts
+const report = new Report()
+  .when(user.isAdmin(), (report) => report.add("Admin section"))
+  .unless(filters.length === 0, (report) => report.add("Filtered"), (report) => report.add("All rows"));
+```
+
+- **Truthiness follows PHP.** `"0"`, `""`, `0`, an empty array, `null` and `false` are falsy, and everything else is truthy, including an empty object.
+- **A closure value** is called with the instance, and its result is the value: `report.when((r) => r.lines.length > 0, …)`. A class passed as `value` is not called; it is the value itself.
+- **The result** is what the callback returns. When the callback returns `null` (or nothing), or when no callback runs, the instance is returned, so the chain continues.
+- **A `null` callback** with a truthy value throws PHP's `Error`, `Value of type null is not callable`, as Laravel does.
+
+`defaultValue` is Laravel's `$default`; `default` is a reserved word in JavaScript.
+
+### Named arguments
+
+As every Xaravel method with more than one optional parameter does, `when` and `unless` also accept one options object keyed by Laravel's parameter names. It stands for PHP's named arguments:
+
+```ts
+report.when({ value: user.isAdmin(), callback: (r) => r.add("Admin section") });
+report.unless({ value: ready, default: (r) => r.add("Pending") }); // Laravel: unless($ready, default: …)
+```
+
+An object is read as options only when it is the only argument, is a plain object, has only the keys `value`, `callback` and `default`, has `callback` or `default`, and each of those is a function or `null`. Any other object is the `value` (see [Divergences](#divergences)).
+
+### Async callbacks
+
+`when` and `unless` are dual helpers (DIV-1). They stay synchronous for synchronous callbacks, and return a Promise when the value closure, `callback` or `defaultValue` returns a Promise. The Promise resolves to what the synchronous call would return:
+
+```ts
+const report = await new Report().when(user.isAdmin(), async (r) => r.add(await loadAdminSection()));
+```
+
+- A Promise passed as the condition, or returned by the value closure, is awaited. It is never tested for truthiness.
+- A callback that returns a query builder or another thenable that is not a Promise stays synchronous: `query.when(term, (q) => q.where("title", term))` returns the builder, and the query does not run. When the call is async for another reason, its Promise adopts the thenable, as every Promise does.
+
+## Higher-order calls
+
+With no callback, `when` and `unless` return a `HigherOrderWhenProxy`. It applies the next method call or property read to the instance only when the condition holds:
+
+- `when(value)` holds `value` as the condition. `unless(value)` holds its negation.
+- `when()` and `unless()` hold no condition yet. The next call or read captures one: its result becomes the condition, negated for `unless()`, and the proxy is returned again.
+
+In Laravel you write `$report->when($admin)->add('Admin section')`. In Xaravel the proxy forwards through its `__call(method, parameters)` and `__get(key)` methods, which are Laravel's magic methods ported as explicit ones:
+
+```ts
+report.when(isAdmin).__call("add", ["Admin section"]); // the report's add() result, or the report when !isAdmin
+
+const proxy = report.when().__call("hasLines", []); // captures the condition
+proxy.__call("add", ["Summary"]); // runs only when hasLines() was truthy
+
+report.unless().__get("archived"); // captures !report.archived
+```
+
+When the condition is false, `__call` returns the instance without calling the method, and `__get` returns the instance without reading the property. `condition(value)` sets the proxy's condition, and `negateConditionOnCapture()` makes the next capture negate its result. Both return the proxy.
+
+## Divergences
+
+- **Dual helper (DIV-1).** `when` and `unless` return a Promise only when an async callback (the value closure, `callback` or `defaultValue`) returns one, and a Promise condition is awaited. A thenable that is not a native Promise is a plain value.
+- **No magic members on the proxy (DIV-12).** Laravel's proxy answers any method or property of its target through `__call` / `__get`. Xaravel has no Proxy outside `Model` (AD-7, AD-26), so call `proxy.__call(method, parameters)` and `proxy.__get(key)`. Whether the proxy should get generated delegating members, so that `report.when(isAdmin).add(…)` works, is open (ESC-12.1-1).
+- **`HigherOrderWhenProxy` state (DIV-12).** `condition()` and `negateConditionOnCapture()` keep their method names; the protected state they set is held under other names, as JavaScript has one namespace per object.
+- **The options form overlaps a condition (open, ESC-12.1-3).** `when` / `unless` have no required parameter, so a single plain object shaped like `{ value, callback, default }` is read as options, where Laravel would read a PHP array of that shape as the condition. The narrow rule above keeps every other object a value.
+- **`ConditionableTest` on Eloquent (open, ESC-12.1-2).** Laravel's tests of `when` / `unless` on an Eloquent query builder wait on the Database Component. This is not a behaviour difference.
